.\" play image as /usr/man/CKSUM: `more /usr/man/CKSUM`.
.TH CKSUM 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
cksum \- write a file checksum and byte count
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cksum
[ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cksum
computes a cyclic redundancy check for each named
.I file
(standard input if none is given) and writes, for each one, the
checksum, the number of octets read, and \(em if an operand
named it \(em the file's own pathname, one line per file.  A
run with no operands prints no pathname at all, matching every
other checksum-shaped tool in this tree
.RI ( sum (1)).
Unlike
.IR sum (1),
.I cksum
prints a pathname for EVERY named operand, even a single one.
.PP
The checksum is the CRC used by the POSIX
.I cksum
utility: a 32-bit CRC (polynomial
.BR 04C11DB7 ,
most-significant bit first, initial value zero) computed over
the file's own bytes, then over the octets of the file's length
itself (least significant octet first, stopping the instant the
remaining length is zero \(em an empty file folds in none), and
finally complemented.  This is
.B not
the same algorithm as
.IR zlib 's
CRC-32.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CKSUM  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sum(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if a file named on the command line could
not be opened \(em that file is skipped, a diagnostic naming it
is written to standard error, and the remaining operands are
still processed.
.SH BUGS
None known.
